                "word": "tarentisme",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "574806fe-1ea7-4fea-a07a-5c612623a63d",
                        "entry_id": "71772e12-7656-313b-8550-025502131f38",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "tarentismes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7ba79544-7b0c-388b-833c-d2a2c5c1520a",
                        "entry_id": "71772e12-7656-313b-8550-025502131f38",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Maladie nerveuse qui a régné dans les Pouilles et même dans une partie de l’Italie du XVᵉ au XVIIᵉ siècles, et qui depuis s’est éteinte."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "958ba17e-1ed8-486a-a430-da6b8cc02655",
                                "sense_id": "7ba79544-7b0c-388b-833c-d2a2c5c1520a",
                                "text": "Elle était particulièrement déterminée par la piqûre de la tarentule, mais aussi par celle d’autres insectes, la piqûre ne paraissant agir en ce cas que comme exerçant une influence sur le système nerveux ; elle résultait aussi de l’imitation, la vue des malades affectés de tarentisme développant la maladie chez d’autres personnes ; elle donnait une extrême envie de danser."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "70a175d3-25d4-4737-b97b-bacd77985098",
                        "source_entry_id": "71772e12-7656-313b-8550-025502131f38",
                        "source_sense_id": "7ba79544-7b0c-388b-833c-d2a2c5c1520a",
                        "target_lang_word": "tarantism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "tarantism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "833ea8a9-fefa-385c-8dc7-cf01ec1db645",
                                        "entry_id": "3f897a0f-19c0-3950-9fba-fe5702c2452d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An extreme urge to dance, popularly thought to have been caused by the bite of a tarantula (Lycosa tarantula) and prevalent in southern Italy in the 15th through 17th centuries."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1d294390-d086-4218-a83b-ae4d6c815332",
                                                "sense_id": "833ea8a9-fefa-385c-8dc7-cf01ec1db645",
                                                "text": "This dancing mania or tarantism prevailed during the whole of the 17th century — and Baglivi, one of the best physicians of that time, made it the subject of a dissertation. He supports his history of the symptoms by the testimony of his father, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79bbc2b5-13c1-427d-9c73-c7c760e6dadb",
                                                "sense_id": "833ea8a9-fefa-385c-8dc7-cf01ec1db645",
                                                "text": "The position depicted is often seen to characterize rituals of tarantism, used for centuries in southern Italy and elsewhere to treat victims of the tarantula's bite, expressing personal and social crises (De Martino 2005)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e354eea7-1f31-4651-adad-230121f9a55b",
                        "source_entry_id": "71772e12-7656-313b-8550-025502131f38",
                        "source_sense_id": "7ba79544-7b0c-388b-833c-d2a2c5c1520a",
                        "target_lang_word": "choreomania",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "choreomania",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "390275af-dd5b-331c-91ae-8621ecd4b381",
                                        "entry_id": "4a61f830-983d-3522-b264-eb2bbe120bfc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of tarantism (“dance mania; dance epidemic”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d45b445a-54f6-45a2-9583-c42d9af611ad",
                                                "sense_id": "390275af-dd5b-331c-91ae-8621ecd4b381",
                                                "text": "In full view of the public, this is the apogee of the choreomania that tormented Strasbourg for a midsummer month in 1518. Also known as the “dancing plague”, it was the most fatal and best documented of the more than ten such contagions which had broken out along the Rhine and Moselle rivers since 1374."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
